# TypeScript Rules

## Strict Mode is Enabled

This project uses strict TypeScript. Never use `any` without justification.

## Import Aliases

```typescript
// TEACHING: Always use path aliases for cleaner imports
// Configured in tsconfig.json: "@/*" → "src/*"

// GOOD
import { db } from "@/server/db";
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";

// BAD - relative paths are harder to refactor
import { db } from "../../../server/db";
```

## Type Definitions

```typescript
// TEACHING: Prefer 'type' for object shapes, 'interface' for extendable contracts
// Types can do unions, interfaces can be augmented

// Use type for data shapes
type Event = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  date: Date;
};

// Use interface for contracts that might be extended
interface Repository<T> {
  find(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  save(item: T): Promise<void>;
}

// TEACHING: Use Zod schemas for runtime validation + type inference
import { z } from "zod";

const EventSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(1, "Title is required"),
  date: z.coerce.date(),
  description: z.string().optional(),
});

// TEACHING: Infer types from Zod schemas - single source of truth
type EventInput = z.infer<typeof EventSchema>;
```

## Handling Nullability

```typescript
// TEACHING: TypeScript strict mode requires explicit null handling
// This prevents "cannot read property of undefined" errors

// BAD - assumes user exists
function getUserName(user: User | null) {
  return user.name; // Error: user might be null
}

// GOOD - handle the null case
function getUserName(user: User | null) {
  return user?.name ?? "Anonymous";
}

// GOOD - early return pattern
function getUserName(user: User | null) {
  if (!user) return "Anonymous";
  return user.name; // TypeScript knows user is not null here
}
```

## Generics

```typescript
// TEACHING: Generics let you write reusable, type-safe code
// The type flows through your function

// TEACHING: Simple generic - T is inferred from usage
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}

const num = first([1, 2, 3]); // type: number | undefined
const str = first(["a", "b"]); // type: string | undefined

// TEACHING: Constrained generic - T must have certain properties
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

1. **Never use `any`** - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
2. **Never use `!` (non-null assertion)** without certainty
3. **Always handle Promise rejections** with try/catch or .catch()
4. **Never ignore TypeScript errors** - fix them or understand why
5. **Use `satisfies`** for type checking without widening
